{
	title: 'Use one shared chrome implementation for every web card',
	spec: 'Every web transcript card uses one shared implementation for its timestamp, reference, actions, header geometry and card expansion controls. All timestamp starts align at the same horizontal inset on desktop and mobile, with no disclosure triangles or reserved triangle indentation anywhere inside cards. Cover prompts, replies, thinking, tools, background results, prompt-file changes, queued and optimistic messages, commands, notices, dividers and active or completed questions. Kind-specific renderers supply content and metadata, not their own timestamp buttons, reference markup or card-toggle behavior. Preserve content-first thinking with timestamp and metadata on its first content row, grouped change summaries including time ranges, existing colors, previews, streaming, folding state and native form semantics. Use the existing CardHeader as the starting point, factoring only the shared shell and disclosure control needed to serve inline and headed layouts. Card expansion has one event policy; independent nested command/raw-output disclosures share the triangle-free control but retain independent state. Preserve keyboard activation, accessible names and aria-expanded, touch targets, links, text selection and reader-owned scroll position. Remove superseded markup and CSS rather than retaining competing paths. This replaces the special headerless prompt-change rendering rule in ar only for shared chrome ownership, not its compact content layout. Verify a real-browser matrix of every card kind, short and long where applicable, at mobile and desktop widths: timestamp x-coordinate parity, absence of disclosure glyphs and their indentation, keyboard and pointer activation, links/actions not toggling the card, selection, and nested disclosure independence. Keep a single compact browser regression for shared chrome behavior. The implementation must not add per-card timers, duplicate observers, or repeated history scans.',
